# @NAME@ — ESPboy

Build: `pio run`. Serial upload: `pio run -t upload` (a ROM larger than
256 KiB also has `rom.bin`, flashed with it at `0x100000`:
`esptool.py write_flash 0x00000 firmware.bin 0x100000 rom.bin`).

| Flash offset | Contents | Limit |
|---|---|---|
| `0x000000` | `.pio/build/espboy/firmware.bin`: Arduino core, runtime, code, ROM ≤ 256 KiB or bank 0 | 1 MiB |
| `0x100000` | `rom.bin`: sectors 32 on of a larger ROM, raw or LZSS (absent for a small ROM) | 2816 KiB |
| `0x3C0000` | cartridge RAM (two copies), save states | 236 KiB |
| `0x3FB000` | Arduino/SDK: EEPROM, RF calibration, Wi-Fi settings | 20 KiB |

ESP8266/LX106, ESP-12F with 4 MiB flash, 128×128 ST7735. Runs at 160 MHz; the
shared menu also offers 80 MHz. No SD; Wi-Fi is switched off. The linker bounds
the application region and a post-build check bounds the upload binary.

Game Boy DMG cartridges only. The recompiler, interpreter, mapper, PPU, APU,
palettes, input/menu and vertical scaling are shared with META/Pokitto.
Horizontal output reduces 160 to 128 without cropping; frames are sent as
strips without a framebuffer. Native ARM assembly and `--meta-thumb` are
unavailable; the firmware compiler is GCC.

ROM storage depends on its size: up to 256 KiB it is part of the firmware and
read in place. Above, bank 0 stays there and every other sector is read with
`spi_flash_read` into the runtime's sector cache; the sectors are raw as long as
they fit the 2816 KiB area, and otherwise only enough of them are LZSS
compressed to fit (largest savings first). A cartridge that stays too big is
rejected at generation. `--meta-pack` has no effect, `--meta-stream on` is
rejected.

Saves: battery RAM (at most 16 KiB) is written as a whole new copy, alternating
between two, with a checksummed header written last; it is written 2 s after
the game stops changing it, when the game disables its RAM, and when leaving
through the menu — never periodically, to spare the flash. States use as many
slots as the save area holds; a state is refused if its checksum does not
match. Saves are tied to the ROM's content: another game flashed later does not
see them (and overwrites them on its first save).

ESC = A, ACT = B (crossed on purpose, more comfortable to play), right auxiliary = Start, left auxiliary tap = Select; hold
the left auxiliary for the shared menu. Quit resets the ESP8266.

Wiring (from the earlier emulator): SPI MOSI GPIO13 / SCK GPIO14, LCD DC
GPIO16, MCP23017 at `0x20` (buttons on port A, LCD chip select GPB0), I²C SDA
GPIO4 / SCL GPIO5, MCP4725 backlight at `0x60`, speaker GPIO0 (sigma-delta
generator at 312.5 kHz, fed at 11025 Hz by timer1). Panel offsets (2, 3),
MADCTL `0xC8`.

The emulation runs inside `setup()` on the core's continuation stack (6 KiB);
the watchdogs are disabled.

Validation is host differential testing, a mock-flash test of the save code,
and Xtensa compilation/size checks. Display, input, sound and saves still need
verification on an actual ESPboy.
